Kind: widen
Issue: #1016
Holds open: the time between a tool's first stop signal and its run's end, inside _stop() -- it holds 100 ms after saying the signal and before it raises, so that the second SIGTERM that systemctl stop sends, 2-3 ms after the first, comes while the handler runs, every time, where without it it came there in 20 stops of 40 on omen; the fixed tree, which ignores the signals from the first, must still take one and pass
Premise: none -- its hold is on the path of every stop the check makes, and nothing a boot does can skip it: the line each tool on run_lock.py must say once, written just before the hold, says the path was driven

diff --git a/scripts/run_lock.py b/scripts/run_lock.py
--- a/scripts/run_lock.py
+++ b/scripts/run_lock.py
@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@
 # Imported, with sys.dont_write_bytecode set first: nothing is to be left in
 # scripts/.
 
-import ctypes, os, signal, subprocess, sys
+import ctypes, os, signal, subprocess, sys, time
 
 LOCK = "/tmp/uros-x86_64-run.lock"
 PR_SET_PDEATHSIG = 1		# prctl(2): a signal for when the parent dies
@@ -131,6 +131,7 @@ def _stop(signum, _frame):
         os.write(2, f"{_who}: stopped by {signal.Signals(signum).name}\n".encode())
     except OSError:
         pass
+    time.sleep(0.1)
     raise SystemExit(128 + signum)
 
 
